・Various Mixup Strategy By using various mix-up methods, we tried to make it impossible to choose unnatural pairs. Details of the method are described below.
・Geometrical Mixup Assuming that geographically close samples should have similar background noise, we created a model that mixes up geographically close samples without learning the background noise.
If the distance matrix is properly constructed, the amount of calculation will be O(n^2), so the code is written so that the parts that match up to the second decimal place of the longitude and latitude are treated as the same group.
・Resonance Mixup (Inspired by collaborative filtering) Choosing a pair that resonates easily as a mixup destination is important for creating a more natural sound. Implemented a mixup that preferentially selects bird samples that are likely to resonate based on the collaborative filtering idea of the recommender system.
・Inner mixup for geometrical clustering space Groups that are geographically close and have the same label do not change the label characteristics even if they share inputs and mixup. Therefore, we clustered such groups and made it possible to perform Inputmixup freely.
Models My models are based on the BirdCLEF 2021 2nd place solution using backbone, eca_nfnet_l0.
Dataset Bird CLEF 2023 Bird CLEF 2022/2021/2020 (Pretraining) ff1010bird_nocall Zendo data (YOLOv8) CV setup I thought it would be difficult to create a multifold model and check the LB due to resource limitations, so I did a simple test-train split instead of out-of-fold.
For the test data, we built validation by splitting 30 seconds into 5 second intervals and bootstrap sampling to the same size as the LB dataset size (8400 samples).
Furthermore, we found that using only the data with the primary label showed behavior similar to LB, so we excluded the data with the secondary label from the validation and calculated.
